\section{Prompt Version Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{sec:appendix_prompt_sensitivity}

To assess whether the confusability effect is robust to prompt formatting, we replicate the BCS-controlled FewRel experiment (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}) using the v2 prompt (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompts}), which adds format constraints and drops offset requirements relative to v1.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Prompt} & \textbf{Group} & \textbf{Mean Acc.} & \textbf{$\Delta$ (pp)} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
v1 & Low-BCS & 82.3\% & \multirow{2}{*}{$-$31.2} & \multirow{2}{*}{---} \\
v1 & High-BCS & 51.0\% & & \\
\midrule
v2 & Low-BCS & 80.4\% & \multirow{2}{*}{$-$7.9} & \multirow{2}{*}{[$-$5.2, $+$21.5]} \\
v2 & High-BCS & 72.5\% & & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{BCS-controlled FewRel accuracy under v1 vs.\ v2 prompts (80-relation pool, count${}\,=\,8$, $n{=}5$ batches per group, Qwen3-32B). The v2 prompt substantially reduces the confusability gap and eliminates the $\approx$51\% accuracy floor observed under v1. The v2 CI crosses zero due to limited statistical power ($n{=}5$/group); the result is directional rather than statistically conclusive.}
\label{tab:prompt_sensitivity}
\end{table}

Under v1, high-BCS batches converge to $\approx$51\% accuracy regardless of model (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}). Under v2, high-BCS accuracy rises to 72.5\%, eliminating this floor. Low-BCS accuracy is comparable across prompt versions (82.3\% vs.\ 80.4\%), indicating that the prompt change primarily affects the model's handling of confusable types rather than its baseline extraction ability.

The confusability gap narrows from 31.2\,pp (v1) to 7.9\,pp (v2), though the v2 bootstrap CI ([$-$5.2, $+$21.5]; $B{=}10{,}000$) crosses zero, reflecting limited power with $n{=}5$ batches per group. This directional finding suggests that confusability-induced degradation is partially prompt-dependent: stricter output format constraints may reduce the model's tendency toward intra-batch substitution errors, consistent with the sensitivity of LLM extraction to prompt engineering \cite{sclar-sensitivity-2024}.

\end{document}

